WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:07.045
Naomi Bagdonas: Alright, so,
how often do you laugh at work?

00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:11.216
I mean really laugh.

00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:15.804
We asked some of you this question,
and here's what we heard.

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:18.181
"Rarely."

00:00:18.848 --> 00:00:20.016
"Seldomly."

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:22.310
And then our personal favorite ...

00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:25.063
(Laughter)

00:00:25.105 --> 00:00:28.149
"I study climate change.
Why would I laugh?"

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.944
The two of us teach a class
at Stanford Business School

00:00:30.944 --> 00:00:32.904
about the power of humor in leadership.

00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:36.616
Jennifer Aaker: Turns out that humor
is a teachable skill,

00:00:36.658 --> 00:00:40.203
and one of the most
underappreciated assets at work.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.830
For the record,

00:00:41.871 --> 00:00:45.166
our class on humor
gets the same academic credit

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:47.127
as financial accounting,

00:00:47.168 --> 00:00:48.545
NB: ... which is not a joke.

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:50.297
JA: Here is the problem, though.

00:00:50.338 --> 00:00:53.174
We've all fallen off a humor cliff.

00:00:53.174 --> 00:00:54.634
In a global study,

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:57.512
over a million people
were asked a simple question:

00:00:57.512 --> 00:01:00.515
"Did you smile or laugh a lot, yesterday?"

00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:02.976
When we are kids, the answer is "yes."

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:05.687
Then, right when we enter the workforce,

00:01:05.729 --> 00:01:07.856
the answer becomes "no."

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:11.693
The good news is
things look up again

00:01:11.735 --> 00:01:12.944
around 80.

00:01:12.986 --> 00:01:16.281
(Laughter)

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:20.035
The bad news is the average
life expectancy is 78.

00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:21.494
So ...

00:01:21.536 --> 00:01:22.537
(Laughter)

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:24.331
JA: It's not good
NB: It's not great.

00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:25.832
JA: I’m a behavioral scientist

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:29.336
who spent my career studying
what actually creates happiness in life

00:01:29.377 --> 00:01:30.879
versus what we think.

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:32.547
NB: And I'm a corporate strategist

00:01:32.547 --> 00:01:36.509
who spent my career straddling
the worlds of business and improv comedy.

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:38.261
Which was a wide straddle.

00:01:38.845 --> 00:01:41.681
The two of us are on a mission
to bring humor back.

00:01:41.723 --> 00:01:43.933
Over the last decade,
we have gathered data,

00:01:43.975 --> 00:01:45.226
partnered with comedians

00:01:45.226 --> 00:01:47.312
and talked to leaders about humor.

00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:50.231
One of them was our friend
Connor Diemand-Yauman.

00:01:50.273 --> 00:01:52.776
Connor is the co-CEO of a large nonprofit,

00:01:52.776 --> 00:01:57.697
and in early 2020, just weeks
after the world went into quarantine,

00:01:57.739 --> 00:02:01.951
he's leading his first virtual offsite
with his entire organization.

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:05.747
People are exhausted
and scared, it's tense.

00:02:05.747 --> 00:02:08.291
So Connor shares a few slides

00:02:08.291 --> 00:02:10.752
before passing to another
teammate to speak.

00:02:10.752 --> 00:02:15.048
But when he does, he intentionally
leaves his screen-share on,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:20.220
so his entire organization watches,
thinking that this is a terrible mistake.

00:02:20.261 --> 00:02:22.263
As Connor closes his PowerPoint,

00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:25.558
opens up a Google search,
and types in ... this.

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:29.145
[Things inspirational CEOs
say during hard times]

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:31.564
(Laughter)

00:02:31.606 --> 00:02:34.818
"Things inspirational CEOs say
during hard times."

00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:36.111
(Laughter)

00:02:36.111 --> 00:02:38.279
Everyone laughs.

00:02:38.279 --> 00:02:40.323
It's this beautiful moment of levity,

00:02:40.323 --> 00:02:44.369
and it has real upside for Connor
and for his organization,

00:02:44.369 --> 00:02:45.995
because we know from the research

00:02:45.995 --> 00:02:49.791
that leaders with a good sense of humor
are seen as 27 percent more motivating.

00:02:49.791 --> 00:02:52.752
Their teams are more bonded and creative.

00:02:52.794 --> 00:02:54.754
JA: Also, humor sells.

00:02:55.255 --> 00:02:59.050
Studies show that adding
a lighthearted line into a sales pitch,

00:02:59.092 --> 00:03:01.845
like "My final offer is x ...

00:03:01.886 --> 00:03:03.722
And I'll throw in my pet frog,"

00:03:03.763 --> 00:03:07.308
makes people willing to pay
nearly 20 percent more.

00:03:08.226 --> 00:03:10.729
Really let it sink in,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.981
just how bad that joke is.

00:03:12.981 --> 00:03:14.149
(Laughter)

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.817
NB: "I'll throw in my pet frog."

00:03:15.859 --> 00:03:18.319
JA: The bar is so low.
NB: It's extreme.

00:03:18.361 --> 00:03:19.571
(Laughter)

00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:21.740
NB: Very low.
JA: Could not be lower.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.491
(Laughter)

00:03:23.491 --> 00:03:26.161
JA: Laughter shortens the path
to connection as well.

00:03:26.202 --> 00:03:29.122
So when strangers share a laugh
before a conversation,

00:03:29.164 --> 00:03:32.709
they end up disclosing
more personal information,

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:34.085
so they feel closer.

00:03:34.586 --> 00:03:37.338
You really laughed hard
at that "pet frog" line.

00:03:37.338 --> 00:03:39.174
I already feel close to you.

00:03:39.174 --> 00:03:40.341
Don’t we feel close?

00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:41.676
JA: Yeah, we feel close.

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:43.094
NB: There's a lot there.

00:03:43.094 --> 00:03:45.346
NB: OK, so what's happening
is when we laugh,

00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:48.224
our brains release a cocktail of hormones.

00:03:48.224 --> 00:03:50.018
So we release endorphins,

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:52.437
giving us a similar feeling
to a runner’s high;

00:03:52.437 --> 00:03:55.523
we lower our cortisol,
making us feel calmer;

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:58.818
and we release dopamine,
the same hormone released during sex,

00:03:58.818 --> 00:04:00.653
making us feel more bonded.

00:04:00.695 --> 00:04:01.946
So in essence,

00:04:01.988 --> 00:04:03.823
as far as our brains are concerned,

00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:08.995
laughing is like exercising, meditating
and having sex at the same time.

00:04:09.037 --> 00:04:10.163
(Laughter)

00:04:10.205 --> 00:04:11.664
But logistically easier.

00:04:11.706 --> 00:04:14.542
JA: Also, efficient.
NB: Yes.

00:04:14.584 --> 00:04:15.752
NB: There's a lot to it.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:17.837
Alright, so humor works.

00:04:18.421 --> 00:04:21.132
And the really good news
that we want to talk about today

00:04:21.132 --> 00:04:23.968
is that this humor cliff
is actually easy to fix.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.762
[GLOBAL HUMOR CLIFF]

00:04:25.762 --> 00:04:27.722
(Laughter)

00:04:27.764 --> 00:04:31.100
Isn’t that better?
10 years, but we got there.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.435
Just a little Sharpie.

00:04:32.477 --> 00:04:36.689
OK, the actual good news
is that our lives are full of humor,

00:04:36.689 --> 00:04:38.316
if we know how to look for it.

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:39.651
JA: Here's the secret.

00:04:39.692 --> 00:04:43.154
Don't look for what's funny,
just notice what's true.

00:04:43.696 --> 00:04:46.115
We asked some of you
what's true in your own lives,

00:04:46.115 --> 00:04:48.409
and here's a few things we heard.

00:04:48.451 --> 00:04:51.746
NB: "Since working from home, I only comb
the front part of my hair."

00:04:51.788 --> 00:04:53.998
(Laughter)

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.959
JA: "I only like my own kids ..."

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.001
(Laughter)

00:04:57.043 --> 00:04:58.419
“There, I said it.”

00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:01.005
NB: So this is not
about becoming a comedian.

00:05:01.047 --> 00:05:03.800
This is about looking at the world
in a different way.

00:05:03.842 --> 00:05:06.678
There's a psychological principle
called the priming effect

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:11.516
that says our brains are wired to see
what we've been set up to expect.

00:05:11.558 --> 00:05:14.310
In essence, we find
what we choose to look for.

00:05:14.352 --> 00:05:17.605
So when we live our lives
on the precipice of a smile,

00:05:17.647 --> 00:05:19.607
we shift how we interact with the world,

00:05:19.607 --> 00:05:21.609
and in turn, how it interacts back.

00:05:21.943 --> 00:05:23.695
JA: It's also about being human,

00:05:23.736 --> 00:05:27.949
which should be easy,
because we are human.

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:30.577
But at work, it's oftentimes
harder than we think.

00:05:30.577 --> 00:05:35.832
In a recent survey, people were asked
"What traits inspire trust in a leader?"

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:39.669
One of the top responses:
"Speaks like a regular person."

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.755
When work gets serious and life gets busy,

00:05:42.797 --> 00:05:44.382
we become transactional.

00:05:44.424 --> 00:05:46.342
And yet these small shifts are enough

00:05:46.342 --> 00:05:49.095
to move us from transactional to human.

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:51.472
As small as a single word,

00:05:51.514 --> 00:05:54.601
like how we often
sign off our emails: “Best.”

00:05:55.226 --> 00:05:56.895
What does "best" even mean?

00:05:56.936 --> 00:05:59.230
Are you the best? Am I the best?

00:05:59.272 --> 00:06:01.190
Why do I need to tell you that?

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.026
We can do better than "best."

00:06:03.026 --> 00:06:05.153
Instead, how about ...

00:06:05.153 --> 00:06:07.113
NB: When you've been up all night --

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:08.489
"Yours heavily caffeinated."

00:06:08.531 --> 00:06:09.949
(Laughter)

00:06:09.991 --> 00:06:12.452
JA: When you're talking
about financial accounting?

00:06:12.493 --> 00:06:14.704
"Let's never speak of this again."

00:06:14.704 --> 00:06:17.332
NB: And on rare occasion, "I'm the best."

00:06:17.373 --> 00:06:18.625
(Laughter)

00:06:18.666 --> 00:06:21.294
Sometimes, you've just
got to let people know.

00:06:21.336 --> 00:06:23.087
Now, it's easy to believe

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:26.507
that if we take our lives
or our mission seriously,

00:06:26.549 --> 00:06:28.843
the presence of humor
betrays that mission.

00:06:28.885 --> 00:06:31.763
That gravity and levity
are somehow at odds.

00:06:31.804 --> 00:06:33.932
But our research tells a different story.

00:06:33.932 --> 00:06:36.559
JA: We met with Secretary of State
Madeleine Albright,

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:39.062
who told us of the time
when the Russian government

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:41.314
had bugged the US State Department,

00:06:41.314 --> 00:06:43.983
a serious breach
in international diplomacy.

00:06:44.692 --> 00:06:46.319
After learning about the bugging,

00:06:46.361 --> 00:06:50.239
Secretary Albright
then went to her next meeting

00:06:50.281 --> 00:06:52.075
with the Russian foreign minister,

00:06:52.075 --> 00:06:53.785
wearing a bug pin.

00:06:53.826 --> 00:06:54.994
(Laughter)

00:06:55.036 --> 00:06:57.455
And enormous bug pin.

00:06:57.455 --> 00:07:04.462
(Laughter)

00:07:05.213 --> 00:07:08.967
She shared how the foreign minister
couldn't help but smile,

00:07:09.008 --> 00:07:11.928
and she also shared
how the energy in the room shifted,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.555
and it changed the conversation entirely.

00:07:15.223 --> 00:07:18.518
NB: This story illuminates
at least two things that are true.

00:07:18.518 --> 00:07:21.187
First, that humor is a choice,

00:07:21.229 --> 00:07:24.315
one we make in small moments
and in big ones, too.

00:07:24.315 --> 00:07:27.276
And second, that the balance
of gravity and levity

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:28.695
gives power to both.

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:32.532
We can do serious things
without taking ourselves so seriously,

00:07:32.532 --> 00:07:35.076
and in fact, often, we can do them better,

00:07:35.076 --> 00:07:36.494
and more fashionably.

00:07:37.412 --> 00:07:40.289
JA: Now we often hear that humor
can be so tricky these days,

00:07:40.331 --> 00:07:41.457
and it's true.

00:07:41.916 --> 00:07:43.084
Comedians know this,

00:07:43.126 --> 00:07:45.378
which is why they can practice
hundreds of times

00:07:45.378 --> 00:07:48.172
before a line makes it
into one of their sets.

00:07:48.214 --> 00:07:50.758
They also get to wear jeans
and drink whiskey at work.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.176
It's all very unfair.

00:07:52.635 --> 00:07:56.055
So how can we, as normal,
non-comedian people,

00:07:56.097 --> 00:07:58.057
practice safe sets?

00:07:58.891 --> 00:08:01.436
Start by recognizing it's not about you.

00:08:01.436 --> 00:08:04.480
So don't ask "Will this
make me sound funny?"

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.484
Instead, ask: “How will this make
other people feel?”

00:08:08.776 --> 00:08:10.778
NB: Which also means never punching down,

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.531
that is, making fun
of someone of lower status.

00:08:13.573 --> 00:08:15.366
And be sure to check your distance.

00:08:15.408 --> 00:08:18.286
How close are you personally
to what you're making light of?

00:08:18.327 --> 00:08:21.748
I can make fun of my mother,
but not your mother,

00:08:21.789 --> 00:08:25.251
who, by the way,
I hear is a saint, a lovely lady.

00:08:25.293 --> 00:08:26.461
JA: An angel.

00:08:27.211 --> 00:08:31.466
NB: Our hope today is that you start
climbing back up the humor cliff.

00:08:31.507 --> 00:08:34.218
Start with what's true, and start small.

00:08:34.218 --> 00:08:37.013
Choose to live
on the precipice of a smile.

00:08:37.055 --> 00:08:40.850
When we do, we create teams,
communities and families

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.311
where joy and laughter come more easily.

00:08:43.811 --> 00:08:46.731
JA: Laughing together
makes us feel more connected.

00:08:46.731 --> 00:08:47.940
It floods our brains

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.151
with the same hormones
associated with love.

00:08:50.777 --> 00:08:54.530
And who doesn't want to feel
more joy and love in our lives?

00:08:54.572 --> 00:08:57.617
Where humor exists,
love is not far behind.

00:08:58.493 --> 00:09:01.329
NB: And if that doesn't sell you
on the power of humor,

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.955
I will throw in my pet frog.

00:09:02.955 --> 00:09:05.041
(Laughter)

00:09:05.041 --> 00:09:06.876
NB: Thank you.
JA: Thank you so much.

00:09:06.918 --> 00:09:10.421
(Applause)

